WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.955
A lab labels each sample with

2
00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:08.589
a code of three letters

3
00:00:08.589 --> 00:00:13.118
taken from the four letters A, C, G and T.

4
00:00:13.118 --> 00:00:16.700
For example, G, A, T, or T, T, A.

5
00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:18.394
To start smaller

6
00:00:18.473 --> 00:00:20.608
here are all the codes with

7
00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:23.850
only two letters, one code in each cell.

8
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:27.691
How many three-letter codes are there?

9
00:00:27.691 --> 00:00:28.978
And how many

10
00:00:28.978 --> 00:00:31.918
if no letter may be used twice?

11
00:00:31.918 --> 00:00:33.480
Pause and guess.

12
00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:38.399
A code is a list

13
00:00:38.399 --> 00:00:41.393
its entries come in a definite order.

14
00:00:41.393 --> 00:00:44.939
A then C, and C then A, are different lists

15
00:00:44.939 --> 00:00:47.460
so they sit in different cells.

16
00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:50.710
Entries may repeat, like A then A

17
00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:52.331
down the diagonal.

18
00:00:52.416 --> 00:00:54.719
The length of a list is the

19
00:00:54.804 --> 00:00:56.681
number of its entries

20
00:00:56.766 --> 00:00:59.069
and the list of length zero

21
00:00:59.155 --> 00:01:00.690
is the empty list.

22
00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:04.180
How many two-letter codes are there?

23
00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:07.494
The first letter picks a row: four ways.

24
00:01:07.494 --> 00:01:08.868
Whatever it was

25
00:01:08.868 --> 00:01:11.616
the second letter picks a column

26
00:01:11.616 --> 00:01:12.990
four ways again.

27
00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:16.712
Four rows times four columns: sixteen cells

28
00:01:16.712 --> 00:01:17.861
sixteen codes.

29
00:01:17.861 --> 00:01:20.773
This is the multiplication principle.

30
00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:24.145
When the number of choices at each position

31
00:01:24.222 --> 00:01:27.134
doesn't depend on the earlier choices

32
00:01:27.134 --> 00:01:27.900
multiply.

33
00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:30.228
Now add a third letter.

34
00:01:30.228 --> 00:01:32.736
Each two-letter code, like G, A

35
00:01:32.736 --> 00:01:36.080
can be followed by any of the four letters.

36
00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:38.588
So there are sixteen times four

37
00:01:38.588 --> 00:01:40.640
which is sixty-four codes.

38
00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:41.959
In general

39
00:01:41.959 --> 00:01:45.307
the lists of length k from n objects

40
00:01:45.307 --> 00:01:49.183
with repetition allowed, number n to the k.

41
00:01:49.183 --> 00:01:52.619
Each of the k positions has n choices

42
00:01:52.619 --> 00:01:54.910
whatever the others hold.

43
00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:59.408
Without repetition, the diagonal goes: A A

44
00:01:59.408 --> 00:02:01.049
C C, G G and T T.

45
00:02:01.049 --> 00:02:03.145
Twelve codes are left

46
00:02:03.145 --> 00:02:06.334
four choices for the first letter

47
00:02:06.334 --> 00:02:09.250
then only three for the second.

48
00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.146
A third letter must be one

49
00:02:12.244 --> 00:02:14.594
of the two not used yet

50
00:02:14.594 --> 00:02:17.923
so twelve times two, twenty-four.

51
00:02:17.923 --> 00:02:22.329
In general: n, times n minus one, and so on

52
00:02:22.329 --> 00:02:23.406
k factors.

53
00:02:23.406 --> 00:02:26.442
This is the falling factorial

54
00:02:26.442 --> 00:02:28.400
n to the k falling.

55
00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:32.649
A tempting shortcut: sixty-four minus four

56
00:02:32.739 --> 00:02:35.278
removing only the codes with

57
00:02:35.369 --> 00:02:37.545
all three letters equal.

58
00:02:37.545 --> 00:02:39.539
But a code like G, A

59
00:02:39.539 --> 00:02:41.715
G repeats a letter too.

60
00:02:41.715 --> 00:02:45.070
Forty codes have a repeat, not four.

61
00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:47.141
When k equals n

62
00:02:47.141 --> 00:02:50.403
every object is used exactly once

63
00:02:50.403 --> 00:02:51.894
an arrangement.

64
00:02:51.894 --> 00:02:55.062
There are n factorial of them: n

65
00:02:55.062 --> 00:02:58.044
times n minus one, down to one.

66
00:02:58.044 --> 00:03:01.679
Four letters in a row: four factorial

67
00:03:01.679 --> 00:03:02.890
twenty-four.

68
00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:05.257
Zero factorial is one

69
00:03:05.348 --> 00:03:07.915
because there is exactly one

70
00:03:08.006 --> 00:03:11.306
list of length zero, the empty list.

71
00:03:11.306 --> 00:03:14.881
And n to the k falling is n factorial

72
00:03:14.881 --> 00:03:17.355
over n minus k, factorial

73
00:03:17.355 --> 00:03:21.480
twelve is four factorial over two factorial.

74
00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:23.583
If k is bigger than n

75
00:03:23.583 --> 00:03:25.495
there is no way at all.

76
00:03:25.575 --> 00:03:27.805
Four letters can't fill five

77
00:03:27.885 --> 00:03:29.797
places without a repeat

78
00:03:29.797 --> 00:03:31.550
so the count is zero.

79
00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:36.429
One warning about the multiplication principle.

80
00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:39.721
Count the codes whose second letter

81
00:03:39.813 --> 00:03:42.374
comes later in the alphabet.

82
00:03:42.374 --> 00:03:45.392
After A there are three choices

83
00:03:45.392 --> 00:03:49.050
after C two, after G one, after T none.

84
00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:52.171
Three plus two plus one plus zero

85
00:03:52.171 --> 00:03:53.067
six codes.

86
00:03:53.149 --> 00:03:56.083
The number of second choices changes

87
00:03:56.164 --> 00:03:57.957
with the first letter

88
00:03:57.957 --> 00:04:00.320
so there is no product here.

89
00:04:00.402 --> 00:04:03.988
Multiply only when every first choice leaves

90
00:04:04.069 --> 00:04:06.840
the same number of second choices.

91
00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:10.334
So: a list keeps its order and

92
00:04:10.432 --> 00:04:12.277
may repeat entries.

93
00:04:12.374 --> 00:04:15.289
With repetition there are n to

94
00:04:15.386 --> 00:04:17.717
the k lists of length k.

95
00:04:17.717 --> 00:04:21.603
Without repetition, n to the k falling

96
00:04:21.603 --> 00:04:25.780
and n factorial when every object is used.

97
00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:30.170
Try it yourself: how many four-digit PINs

98
00:04:30.263 --> 00:04:32.400
have no repeated digit?

99
00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:33.686
Next, sets

100
00:04:33.771 --> 00:04:37.004
collections where order and repetition

101
00:04:37.089 --> 00:04:38.790
don't matter at all.
